The requirement analysis phase defined exactly what the system must do and under what conditions. Functionally, the system must allow users to register and log in, choose a document type, answer a dynamic set of questions, receive an AI-generated draft, read a plain-language explanation of each clause, edit the draft, and export it as a PDF or DOCX file. Users must also be able to view their saved documents and earlier versions from a dashboard. Non-functional requirements include fast performance, with a draft generated in under thirty seconds, strong security through HTTPS, encryption of stored data, and hashed passwords, and a responsive, accessible interface that non-technical users can operate without help. The system should also be reliable and scalable enough to handle many users at once. On the technical side, the project needs a frontend framework such as React or plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, a backend built with Python or Node.js, a database such as PostgreSQL, MongoDB, or Firebase, an LLM API for text generation, and tools such as Git, VS Code, Postman, and Figma. The main assumptions are that users provide accurate information and treat the output as a draft, and the main constraint is that the tool cannot replace professional legal advice.
